  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "If the absolute value of the difference between the distances equals the distance between the two foci, what is the locus?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "A hyperbola",
    "B": "An ellipse",
    "C": "A circle",
    "D": "Two rays"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Assume the foci \\(F_1,F_2\\) are distinct, as recorded, and let \\(d=F_1F_2>0\\). The condition is \\(|PF_1-PF_2|=d\\). Comparing the three distances helps determine where \\(P\\) can lie.\n\nIf \\(P\\) is not on the line through the foci, the triangle-side inequality gives \\(|PF_1-PF_2|<d\\), excluding such points. If \\(P\\) lies strictly between the foci, both focal distances are positive and their sum is \\(d\\), so their absolute difference is also less than \\(d\\).\n\nOn either outward extension of the focal segment, the longer focal distance equals the shorter distance plus \\(d\\), so the required equality holds. It also holds at each focus. Thus the locus is two outward rays, each including its starting focus: choice D.\n\nThe distinct-focus condition matters: if the foci coincided, every point would satisfy \\(0=0\\), giving the whole plane.",
